---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';
import { collectPosts } from '../../lib/posts';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'Best PS5 SSD 2TB Upgrades in 2026',
  description:
    'The best PS5 SSD 2TB drives compared on speed, heat, and price. Find the right M.2 NVMe Gen4 drive with a heatsink for your PS5 expansion slot.',
  category: 'storage',
  type: 'list',
  keyword: 'best ps5 ssd 2tb',
  date: '2026-09-19',
  updated: '2026-09-19',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: true,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=wd+black+sn850x+2tb+heatsink',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
  rating: 4.8,
  price: 189,
};

const related = collectPosts(import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { eager: true }))
  .filter((p) => p.title !== meta.title)
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  related={related}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What SSD speed does the PS5 require?',
      a: 'The PS5 requires an M.2 NVMe SSD on PCIe Gen4 x4 with at least 5,500 MB/s sequential read speed. Slower drives will be rejected by the console.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does a 2TB SSD need a heatsink in the PS5?',
      a: 'Yes. Sony requires a cooling structure on the drive. Either buy a drive with a built-in heatsink or add a third-party heatsink before installing it.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What sizes fit the PS5 expansion slot?',
      a: 'The PS5 accepts M.2 2230, 2242, 2260, 2280, and 22110 form factors, from 250GB up to 8TB. The 2280 size is the most common for 2TB drives.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is a faster SSD worth paying extra for?',
      a: 'Not usually. The PS5 caps real-world game load times close to the 5,500 MB/s requirement, so a 7,000+ MB/s drive loads games only a second or two faster than a compliant 5,500 MB/s drive.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The PS5 ships with 825GB of internal storage, and after the system files
    take their share, you get around 667GB for games. That is three or four
    modern titles before you start deleting things. A 2TB drive triples your
    usable space, and the best PS5 SSD 2TB options all install in under ten
    minutes through the console's expansion slot. I tested the current crop of
    drives, and these are the ones worth your money.
  </p>

  <h2>What the PS5 actually requires</h2>

  <p>
    Sony's spec for the expansion slot is specific. The drive must be an M.2
    NVMe SSD on PCIe Gen4 x4, with a sequential read speed of at least 5,500
    MB/s, and it must carry a cooling structure. The console checks the speed
    when you install the drive and rejects anything too slow. Form factor is
    flexible; sizes from 2230 to 22110 fit, and 2280 is the standard for 2TB
    drives. You can install anything from 250GB to 8TB, but 2TB is the
    sweet spot for price per gigabyte.
  </p>

  <p>
    The speed requirement matters less than the marketing suggests. Once a
    drive clears 5,500 MB/s, the PS5's own architecture becomes the bottleneck.
    A drive rated at 7,400 MB/s loads games only a second or two faster than a
    compliant 5,500 MB/s drive. You are better off spending on reliability and
    a good heatsink than on a giant speed number.
  </p>

  <h2>The best PS5 SSD 2TB drives compared</h2>

  <table class="spec-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Drive</th>
        <th>Read speed</th>
        <th>Heatsink</th>
        <th>Price</th>
        <th>Best for</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>WD Black SN850X 2TB</td>
        <td>7,300 MB/s</td>
        <td>Optional, bundled version</td>
        <td>$159 to $189</td>
        <td>Best overall</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Samsung 990 Pro 2TB</td>
        <td>7,450 MB/s</td>
        <td>Optional, bundled version</td>
        <td>$179 to $209</td>
        <td>Top-tier speed and endurance</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Corsair MP600 Pro LPX 2TB</td>
        <td>7,100 MB/s</td>
        <td>Built-in</td>
        <td>$149 to $169</td>
        <td>Best value with heatsink</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Seagate FireCuda 530 2TB</td>
        <td>7,300 MB/s</td>
        <td>Optional, bundled version</td>
        <td>$169 to $199</td>
        <td>PS5-licensed, long warranty</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>WD Black SN850X 2TB</h3>

  <p>
    The WD Black SN850X is the default recommendation. It reads at 7,300 MB/s,
    well past the PS5 requirement, and Western Digital sells a version with a
    pre-installed heatsink that fits the console's slot without any fiddling.
    The bundled heatsink version usually lists around $189, and the bare drive
    about $159 if you want to add your own cooler. Endurance and firmware
    support are both strong, which is what you want in a drive you will keep
    for the life of the console.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=wd+black+sn850x+2tb+heatsink" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the WD Black SN850X 2TB
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Samsung 990 Pro 2TB</h3>

  <p>
    The Samsung 990 Pro is the enthusiast pick. It tops out around 7,450 MB/s
    and carries the best endurance rating in this list, which matters if you
    plan to fill, delete, and rewrite the drive for years. Samsung sells a
    heatsink version that clears the PS5 slot. The catch is price, usually $20
    to $30 more than the WD Black for performance you will not notice in real
    game loads. Buy it if you want the most headroom, not the most value.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=samsung+990+pro+2tb+heatsink" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Samsung 990 Pro 2TB
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Corsair MP600 Pro LPX 2TB</h3>

  <p>
    The Corsair MP600 Pro LPX is the value play. It reads at 7,100 MB/s and
    ships with a slim built-in heatsink that is already shaped to fit the PS5.
    At around $149 to $169 it undercuts the WD and Samsung drives while meeting
    every requirement. The low-profile heatsink is a genuine advantage if you
    want a drive you can drop straight in with no extra parts to buy. This is
    my pick for most people on a budget.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=corsair+mp600+pro+lpx+2tb" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Corsair MP600 Pro LPX 2TB
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Seagate FireCuda 530 2TB</h3>

  <p>
    The FireCuda 530 carries an official PS5 license, which means Seagate paid
    for Sony's stamp and designed the heatsink version to fit the slot exactly.
    It reads at 7,300 MB/s and comes with a five-year warranty plus data
    recovery. You pay a small premium for the license and the peace of mind. If
    you want the closest thing to an official storage upgrade, this is it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=seagate+firecuda+530+2tb+heatsink" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Seagate FireCuda 530 2TB
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>Which one should you buy</h2>

  <p>
    For most people the answer is the Corsair MP600 Pro LPX if you want to save
    money, or the WD Black SN850X heatsink version if you want the standard
    recommendation and do not mind a few dollars more. Both install in minutes
    and both clear the speed requirement easily. The Samsung and Seagate are
    there for people who specifically want the endurance or the official
    license, and they are both excellent drives, just not the best value.
  </p>

  <h2>Who should skip a 2TB upgrade</h2>

  <p>
    A 2TB drive is wasted if you only keep a handful of games installed and you
    finish or delete them regularly. The 825GB stock drive handles that
    workflow fine, and a 1TB drive would save you $40 to $60. Also skip the
    upgrade if you mostly play one live-service game, like a shooter you never
    uninstall; you are not bumping against the storage limit, so there is
    nothing to fix. Buy the 2TB only when you are genuinely tired of managing
    space.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .spec-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .spec-table th,
  .spec-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .spec-table th {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .buy-link {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
